  "text": "All three solutions give the same value, min(ad, d), and I agree with it. Solutions 2 and 3 use the requested min(x, y) form directly, and Solution 1's d·min(a,1) is the same function.\n\n**Lower bound.** For any pair with ‖x‖ = ‖y‖ = 1 and ‖x − y‖ = d′ ≥ d, take z = −y·1_E. Then ‖x+z‖ − ‖y+z‖ = ∫_E g dμ, where g = |x−y| + |y| − |x| ≥ 0 and ∫g = d′. The cost is ∫_E |y|. Because μ is atomless, the best gain for budget a is a fractional knapsack value G(a), which is concave with G(0) = 0. Let C′ = ∫_{g>0}|y| ≤ 1, so G(C′) = d′. Concavity gives G(a) ≥ a·d′/C′ ≥ a·d for a ≤ C′. For a > C′, G(a) = d′ ≥ d. Either way the supremum is at least min(ad, d).\n\n**Upper bound.** Take y = 1 on [0,1] and x = (1 − d/2) on [0,1] plus 1 on [2, 2 + d/2]. Then ‖x‖ = ‖y‖ = 1 and ‖x − y‖ = d. Pointwise, the gain per unit of ‖z‖ from negative z on [0,1] is at most d, and no gain is possible elsewhere. For a ≤ 1, z = −1 on a set of measure a gives exactly ad. For a ≥ 1 the bound d is attained, since the gain never exceeds ‖x − y‖. So the supremum for this pair equals min(ad, d).\n\nTherefore U_{L₁(μ)}(d; a) = min(ad, d). Since all three solutions reach this value, the choice below is arbitrary among them. I picked Solution 2 because it states the answer in the requested min(x, y) form.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
